In Jetpack Compose, Box is a standard layout composable used to stack elements on top of each other. If you are familiar with traditional Android development, Box serves the same fundamental purpose as a FrameLayout or a relative layout where children are drawn sequentially, meaning later items in the code are drawn on top of earlier ones. Beyond simple overlapping, Box allows you to precisely align individual children within the available space without needing nested linear layouts.
How it works:
When you place composables inside a Box, they are measured and positioned relative to the Box itself rather than to each other. By default, all children are aligned to the top-start corner of the Box. You can change this behavior globally by passing a contentAlignment parameter to the Box, or locally for specific children by wrapping them in the Modifier.align() modifier.
A commonly misunderstood aspect of Box is how it handles sizing. Unlike Column or Row, which measure their children based on available linear space, a Box passes the incoming constraints directly to its children. If a child is smaller than the Box, you can use Modifier.matchParentSize() to force that child to match the size of the Box itself, without affecting how the Box calculates its own dimensions from other unconstrained children.
